      "rev_n_10_ddedb5_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_10_ddedb5_0",
        "node_id": "n_10_ddedb5",
        "thought_text": "Simulate the balance year by year starting from 100 yen, applying the 1% interest and truncating fractions until the balance reaches or exceeds X. Since X can be up to 10^18, the number of years will be around 60-70, making a simple loop efficient enough.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-5184341265383926183",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794505696.3663936
      },
      "rev_n_11_4d14c3_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_11_4d14c3_0",
        "node_id": "n_11_4d14c3",
        "thought_text": "Use binary search on the number of years. Define a function to calculate the balance after N years using a loop, then binary search for the smallest N where the balance is >= X. This avoids simulating every single year if the gap between years is large, though the gap is small here.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-5184341265383926183",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794505696.3664129
      },
      "rev_n_12_9653bc_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_12_9653bc_0",
        "node_id": "n_12_9653bc",
        "thought_text": "Apply the mathematical formula for compound interest: Balance = 100 * (1.01)^N. Solve for N using logarithms: N = ceil(log(X/100) / log(1.01)). Then verify the result by simulating a few steps to account for the integer truncation of fractions, which the formula ignores.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-5184341265383926183",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794505696.3664212
      },
      "rev_n_13_078cf3_0": {
